{
  "headline": "Toronto City Clerk certifies 2026 municipal election results",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Toronto City Clerk John D. Elvidge has certified the official results for Toronto’s 2026 municipal election. The official declaration of results, which indicates the total number of votes received by each candidate, is available on the Toronto Elections Results webpage. Poll-by-poll results, detailing votes cast during advance voting, by mail, and at each election day voting place, are also available through the city's website. Final voter statistics, including the official number of eligible voters following all revisions to the voters’ list, are scheduled to be available within 30 days of the election. The 2026-2030 term of Toronto City Council is set to begin on October 25, 2026. New members of Council will make their Declaration of Office privately with the City Clerk prior to the first meeting of City Council, which is scheduled for October 28, 2026."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Africa CDC urges coordinated action as Ebola outbreak exceeds 10,000 cases",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Africa Centres for Disease Control and Prevention (Africa CDC) has called for strengthened cross-border cooperation and coordinated responses as an Ebola outbreak caused by the Bundibugyo virus spreads beyond the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC). The call was made during an extraordinary virtual meeting of African health ministers on October 28, 2026, to discuss measures for improved surveillance, preparedness, and containment efforts. Africa CDC Director-General Dr. Jean Kaseya stated the outbreak is an economic destabilizer affecting trade, supply chains, and household livelihoods. According to the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), the outbreak had recorded 10,172 confirmed cases and 4,932 confirmed deaths across five affected countries and territories as of October 26. The CDC described the event as the second-largest Ebola outbreak on record, following the 2014–2016 West African epidemic. The DRC has accounted for the overwhelming majority of cases and deaths since the outbreak began in May 2026. Congolese health authorities reported on October 27, 2026, that the country had recorded 10,060 infections and 4,887 deaths, noting the epidemic's fatality rate appears close to 50 percent according to the Congolese National Institute of Public Health. The outbreak was first detected in the northeastern province of Ituri and has since spread across the north and east of the country. Challenges to containment include the lack of approved vaccines or specific treatments for the Bundibugyo variant. The outbreak has also been reported in Kenya, Nigeria, and Uganda, with cases also associated with the wider outbreak in France. In Kenya, nine infections and two deaths have been confirmed. Nigeria confirmed its first case on October 20, involving a 42-year-old Swiss woman who entered from Uganda; she was evacuated to a hospital in Geneva on October 26, 2026. Uganda has recorded 20 confirmed cases traced to a patient from the DRC. During the October 28 meeting, Simbisile Kulu, South Africa’s Director-General for Health, described the spread as a continental health security concern and noted that South Africa is strengthening surveillance, laboratory capacity, and border preparedness."
    }
  ]
}

