{
  "headline": "Electric hydrofoil boats are being deployed in the United States",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "In New York Harbor, Vessev is operating fully electric hydrofoil boats. Eric Laakmann, the chief executive and co-founder of Vessev, described the vessels as a curiosity at the moment. These boats utilize underwater wings to lift their hulls approximately a foot and a half above the water surface as they accelerate, a process Laakmann referred to as a takeoff. This design is intended to dramatically reduce drag, which is a significant obstacle to energy efficiency in maritime travel. Replacing diesel-powered ferries with electric vessels could reduce emissions and improve air quality. However, electrifying boats presents challenges because batteries are heavy, and that additional weight increases the power required to move a vessel."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Biman Bangladesh Airlines flight carrying Prime Minister Tarique Rahman departs Narita for Dhaka after five-hour delay",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A Biman Bangladesh Airlines flight carrying Prime Minister Tarique Rahman and his entourage departed from Narita International Airport in Japan for Dhaka on Thursday, approximately five hours behind schedule, following a technical fault. The aircraft, flight BG 377, took off at around 10:45 pm local time in Japan, according to a member of the prime minister’s entourage who confirmed the information to Prothom Alo. Biman’s public relations department stated in a press release that the flight had safely taken off after the technical fault was repaired by Biman engineers and Japanese technicians. The airline had previously issued a press release around 5:00 pm stating the flight was delayed due to the fault while flight operations and engineering teams worked to resolve the issue. The flight was originally scheduled to depart at approximately 6:00 pm local time on Thursday following a four-day state visit to Tokyo. The aircraft was reported ready for departure at around 10:15 pm local time after repairs were completed. According to the airline, the flight took off at 7:55 pm Bangladesh time and is scheduled to land in Dhaka at 3:00 am on 30 October."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Experts warn US climate forecasting capacity hampered by NOAA cuts amid El Niño risks",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Former government officials and scientists have warned that sweeping cuts to the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) have severely hampered the United States' capacity to forecast, research, and respond to extreme weather events. These warnings come as the country faces potential hazards fueled by a potentially historic El Niño this winter. Since January 2025, the agency has lost approximately 20% of its staff. Under President Donald Trump, the agency has seen slashed staff levels, canceled contracts and grants, and the shutdown of research laboratories and databases. Tom Di Liberto, a former NOAA climate scientist and current media director of Climate Central, stated that the system has not been tested since the cuts occurred and questioned whether the country can handle potential coast-to-coast extremes this winter. The National Weather Service, which is housed within NOAA, has also been affected; weather stations have delayed data collection and cut operating hours, while hundreds of technical and meteorological positions remain vacant across its 122 forecast offices. Within NOAA, reports indicate a volatile environment has led to increased burnout and plummeting morale. This reduction in capacity coincides with a super El Niño, a phenomenon of warmer than average equatorial sea surface temperatures, which is expected to strengthen through the northern hemisphere’s autumn and winter. The phenomenon has the potential to supercharge storms in parts of the American west, raise sea levels in California by up to a foot, and contribute to flash floods, landslides, and debris flows."
    }
  ]
}

